        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.69,
            0.065,
            0.834,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Lines and Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.157,
            0.121,
            0.833,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": "4. Get a slanting crease on the paper. Now, try to get another crease that is perpendicular to the slanting crease."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.169,
            0.833,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": "a. How many right angles do you have now? Justify why the angles are exact right angles."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.196,
            0.215,
            0.834,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "b. Describe how you folded the paper so that any other person who doesn’t know the process can simply follow your description to get the right angle."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.286,
            0.325,
            0.308
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Classifying Angles"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.314,
            0.834,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": "Angles are classified in three groups as shown below. Right angles are shown in the second group. What could be the common feature of the other two groups?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.39,
            0.753,
            0.602
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.622,
            0.834,
            0.667
          ],
          "content": "In the first group, all angles are less than a right angle or in other words, less than a quarter turn. Such angles are called **acute angles**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.668,
            0.834,
            0.736
          ],
          "content": "In the third group, all angles are greater than a right angle but less than a straight angle. The turning is more than a quarter turn and less than a half turn. Such angles are called **obtuse angles**."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.746,
            0.307,
            0.769
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.782,
            0.833,
            0.826
          ],
          "content": "1. Identify acute, right, obtuse and straight angles in the previous figures."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.833,
            0.833,
            0.875
          ],
          "content": "2. Make a few acute angles and a few obtuse angles. Draw them in different orientations."
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
